## Electrochemistry and Electrochemiluminescence from a Redox-Active Metal-Organic Framework
†State Key Laboratory of Medicinal Chemical Biology, Collaborative Innovation Center of Chemical Science and Engineering (Tianjin), and Research Center for Analytical Sciences, College of Chemistry, Nankai University, Tianjin, 300071, China
China
‡ Dalian Institute of Chemical Physics, National Chromatographic R. & A. Center, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Dalian, 116011, China
Abstract
The marriage of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and electrochemiluminescence (ECL) can combine their merits together. Designing ECL-active MOF with a high electron transfer capacity and high stability is critical for ECL emission. Here we reported the ECL from a redox-active MOF prepared from \(\{\mathsf{R u}[4,4^{\prime}-(\mathsf{H O}_{2}\mathsf{C})_{2}-\mathsf{b p y}]_{2}\mathsf{b p y}\}^{2+}\) and \(Z\mathsf{n}^{2+}\); a property of MOFs has not been reported previously. The MOF structure is independent of its charge and is therefore stable electrochemically. The redox-activity and well-ordered porous structure of the MOF were confirmed by its electrochemical properties and ECL emission. The high ECL emission indicated the ease of electron transfer between the MOF and co-reactants. Furthermore, the MOF exhibited permselectivity, charge selectivity, and catalytic selectivity along with a stable and concentration-dependent ECL emission toward co-reactants. ECL mechanism was proposed based on the results. The detection and recovery of cocaine in the serum sample was used to validate the feasibility of MOF-based ECL system. The information obtained in this study provides a better understanding of the redox properties of MOFs and their potential electrochemical applications.
Keywords: Electrochemistry; Electrochemiluminescence; Metal-organic framework; Redox-active complex
## 1. Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted much attention as promising materials for separation and gas storage owing to their permanent nano-scale porosity, high specific surface area and well-defined pore size (Gu et al., 2012, Qiu & Zhu, 2009, Yaghi et al., 2003, Horcajada et al., 2010, Farha & Hupp, 2010, Zhou et al., 2006, Gu & Yan, 2010, Gu, Jiang, et al., 2011, Li et al., 2009, Xie et al., 2011, Chang et al., 2011, Cui et al., 2009, Gu, Chen, et al., 2011, Gu et al., 2010, Yang & Yan, 2011, 2012, Chang et al., 2010). Chemical sensors have been developed with MOF-based materials (Kreno et al., 2012). Bioimaging has been achieved with a few MOFs (Cui et al., 2011, Della Rocca et al., 2011), such as \(\mathsf{G d}^{3+}\)- and \(\mathsf{M n}^{2+}\)-containing MOFs for magnetic resonance imaging (Taylor et al., 2008) and a phosphorescent MOF for optical imaging (Liu, Huxford, et al., 2011). In fact, MOFs also facilitate electrochemical (EC) application because of their high mass transfer capacities in their well-ordered porous structure (Morozan & Jaouen, 2012, Miyasaka, 2012). The catalytic sites in MOFs are readily accessible to enhance electrocatalytic efficiency (Morozan & Jaouen, 2012, Miyasaka, 2012, Yang et al., 2010, Mao et al., 2012, Hosseini et al., 2013, Zhu et al., 2011). Therefore, the potential advantages of integrating MOFs and EC process are significant, but MOF-based EC systems are less advanced.
One of the challenges for MOF-based EC systems is the design of redox-active MOFs with high electron transfer capacities and stability towards redox procedure (Morozan & Jaouen, 2012, Miyasaka, 2012, Yang et al., 2010, Mao et al., 2012, Hosseini et al., 2013, Zhu et al., 2011). Organic ligands have been used to prepare redox-active MOFs (Usov et al., 2012, Wade et al., 2013). The application of redox-active metal complexes represents a strategy for improving electron transfer and responding to EC stimuli of MOFs (Morozan & Jaouen, 2012, Miyasaka, 2012). Redox-active metal complexes are
advantageous for the preparation of EC-active MOFs because they are extensively available, show the stable topology of coordinating sites, and provide the possibility of direct connection between electron donors and acceptors (Morozan & Jaouen, 2012, Miyasaka, 2012).
Electrochemiluminescence (ECL) is attractive because of its low background signal, high sensitivity, and wide dynamic range (Richter, 2004, Hu & Xu, 2010, Yin et al., 2004, Yin, 2012, Wei et al., 2007, Wei & Wang, 2008, Miao, 2008, Forster et al., 2009, Wei et al., 2008, Wei et al., 2008, Liu et al., 2007). Ruthenium complexes, such as \(\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}^{2+}\) and \(\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{p h e n})_{3}^{2+}\) are the most important ECL luminophores and have been used to determine many clinically-important analytes in commercial systems. ECL methods have been developed to detect metal ions, small molecules, and biomacromolecules in our group (Liu et al., 2011a, Yin et al., 2009, Liu et al., 2011b, Gao et al., 2013, Liu et al., 2011, Bu et al., 2013, Wang et al., 2013, Bu et al., 2011, Tang et al., 2011, Tang et al., 2010, Zhao et al., 2011). The immobilization of \(\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}^{2+}\) on a substrate provides additional advantages, including simplified setup, enhanced ECL signal, and reusability (Khramov & Collinson, 2000, Wei & Wang, 2011, Xing & Yin, 2009b, Choi et al., 2003, Sun et al., 2010, Forster & Hogan, 2000, Guo & Dong, 2004, Xing & Yin, 2009a). The marriage of MOFs and ECL detection can combine the merits of both technologies; however, to the best of our knowledge, the MOF-based ECL system has not yet been reported. Herein, we describe the ECL system based on the MOF prepared from \(\{\mathsf{R u}[4,4^{\prime}-(\mathsf{H O}_{2}\mathsf{C})_{2}-\mathsf{b p y}]_{2}\mathsf{b p y}\}^{2+}\) and \(\mathsf{Z n}^{2+}\), which was denoted as Ru-MOF and reported by Kent et al. (Kent et al., 2010). The properties integrated from MOFs and ECL was obviously revealed. ECL emission with good concentration-dependent response toward co-reactants was observed, which has not been reported previously for MOFs. The permselectivity, charge
selectivity and catalytic selectivity of the MOF were demonstrated through its ECL behaviour in the presence of different co-reactants.
## 2. Experimental Section
## 2.1 Instrumentation
The ECL emission was excited using a Model LK98BII Microcomputer-based Electrochemical Analyzer (Tianjin Lanlike High-Tech Company, Tianjin, China). A traditional three-electrode system was used with Ag/AgCl/KCl (saturated) as reference electrode, 3-mm diameter glassy carbon electrode modified with Ru-MOF or \( \mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{Ru}} \) as working electrode, and Pt wire as counter electrode. The ECL emission was detected by using a model MPI-A Electrochemiluminescence Analyzer (Xi'An Remax Electronic Science & Technology Co. Ltd, Xi'An, China) at room temperature with the voltage of the photomultiplier tube (PMT) set at -900 V during the detection procedure. The \( \mathsf{N}_{2} \) adsorption-desorption isotherm was obtained with ASAP2020/Tristar 3000 surface area and pore analyser at 274 K. The pore-size distribution was calculated according to the Horvath-Kawazoe program. The morphology of Ru-MOF were characterized by using a field-emission scanning electron microscope (Nova Nano SEM 230, FEI Co.)
## 2.2 Synthesis of Ru-MOF
2.2 Synthesis of Ru-MOFRu-MOF was synthesized according to Kent et al's work (Kent et al., 2010) and illustrated in Fig. 1. 4,4'-CO\(_2\)Me-bipyridine (denoted as 4,4'-CO\(_2\)Me-bpy, 430 mg, 1.58 mmol) and Ru(cyclo-octadene)Cl\(_2\) (221.5 mg, 0.79 mmol) were added in 20 mL \(o\)-dichlorobenzene with stirring for 2 hours at \(160\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under argon gas condition. Ru(4,4'-CO\(_2\)Me-bpy)2Cl\(_2\) (1) was therefore prepared. 1 (320 mg, 0.45 mmol) and excess bipyridine (114 mg, 0.73 mmol) were refluxed under argon in 10 mL H\(_2\)O:CH\(_3\)CHOH (1:1 v/v) for 8 hours. 10 mL of saturated aqueous ammonium hexafluorophosphate (NH\(_4\)PF\(_6\)) was added into the solution to precipitate product 2. After being collected and washed with ether to remove excess bipyridine, product 2 was hydrolyzed to remove the methyl groups by refluxing under argon in 25 mL 4 M HCl for 12 hours. The red product was further precipitated with 10 mL of saturated aqueous NH\(_4\)PF\(_6\). The product of 3 \(\{\left[\mathrm{Ru}(4,4^{\prime}-\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{H}-\mathrm{bpy})_{2}\mathrm{bpy}\right](\mathrm{PF}_{6})_{2}\}\) was collected after being washed with water to remove excess HCl. Ru-MOF was prepared with 10 mg 3 (denoted as L\(_{\mathrm{Ru}}\)) and 10 mg Zn(NO\(_3\))\(_2\) in 4 mL N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF)/H\(_2\)O (3:1 v/v) with stirring at \(90\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 3 days followed by precipitation and collection from the original solution.
## 2.3 Synthesis of graphene oxide (GO)
2.3 Synthesis of graphene oxide (GO)GO was synthesized with the modified Hummers' method (Jahan et al., 2010, Chen et al., 2012). After 2.0 g graphite was mixed with 5 mL H\(_2\)SO\(_4\) and 1.2 g NaNO\(_3\) in a 100 mL flask, 1.5 g KMnO\(_4\) was added into the solution slowly with an ice bath. The solution was stirred for 2 hours at \(35\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). A 20 mL of water was added and the mixture was stirred at \(98\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 15 min. Finally, 50 mL of water was added, followed by 3 mL of \(30\,\%\) H\(_2\)O\(_2\). The resulted solution was filtered and washed with dilute HCl and water. The obtained GO cake was dispersed in water by being ultrasonicated for 1 hour. The suspension was
centrifuged at 1000 rpm for 10 min to remove the visible particles. The supernatant was collected with centrifuged at 12000 rpm for 10 min.
## 2.4 Preparation of Ru-MOF/GO-modified electrode
The preparation and test of the Ru-MOF/GO modified electrode was illustrated in Scheme 1. 5.0 mg GO was dissolved into 200 μL DMF/H\(_2\)O (3:1, v/v) to form mucus GO solution. Then, 1.0 mg Ru-MOF (MW: 1003, 1 μmol) was added into the GO solution and stirred drastically to form Ru-MOF/GO composition. 5 μL of the Ru-MOF/GO composite solution was dropped onto a glass carbon electrode and dried at room temperature for 12 hours to prepare the Ru-MOF/GO-modified electrode. As a comparison, the \( \mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{Ru}}/\mathsf{GO} \)-modified electrode was prepared with \( \mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{Ru}} \) (1 mg, MW: 1035, 1 μmol) in replace of Ru-MOF in a similar process. The electrochemical and ECL performance of the Ru-MOF/GO-modified electrode was tested in the solution of DMF:H\(_2\)O (3:1 v/v) with different concentrations of TPrA, triethylamine (TEA), or oxalate using three-electrode system. The properties of \( \mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{Ru}}/\mathsf{GO} \)-modified electrode were investigated as comparison. To validate the real application, the MOF-modified electrode was used to detect cocaine in normal saline and serum samples and to test the recovery of cocaine in serum.
