## 3. Results and Discussion
## 3.1 Synthesis and characterization of Ru-MOF
Ru-MOF was synthesized according to Kent et al's work (Kent et al., 2010). As shown in Fig. 1, \( \{\mathsf{Ru}[4,4^{\prime}-(\mathsf{HO}_2\mathsf{C})_2\mathsf{bpy}]_2\mathsf{bpy}\}^{2+} \) (denoted as \( \mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{Ru}} \)) and \( \mathsf{Zn}^{2+} \) were used as the edges and nodes of the MOF, respectively. The entire complex, \( \mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{Ru}} \), was used to prepare Ru-MOF because it has a rigid structure
and four carboxylic groups to coordinate to \( Z\mathsf{n}^{2+} \) ions (Cui et al., 2011, Della Rocca et al., 2011, Kent et al., 2010). Moreover, \( \mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{R u}} \) is EC- and ECL-active in a manner similar to its counterpart, \( \mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}^{2+} \) (Richter, 2004, Hu & Xu, 2010, Yin et al., 2004, Yin, 2012). \( \mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{R u}} \) converts between \( \mathsf{R u}^{\mathsf{I I},\mathsf{I I I}} \) without any structural change (de Ruiter et al., 2013). The structure of Ru-MOF is therefore independent of its charge and stable and favorable for electron transfer procedure.
The preferred position for Fig. 1.
The formation of \( \mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{R u}} \) and Ru-MOF was confirmed by the results in Fig. S1-S3 and Table S1. The rod-like structure of Ru-MOF with a size of approximately \( 220\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\,\times\,30\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\,\times\,10\ \upmu\mathrm{m} \) was observed in their SEM images and the photo of single MOF crystal (Fig. 2A-2C). The deep brick-red color of MOF crystal was the same as that of \( \mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{R u}} \), indicating a high content of \( \mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{R u}} \) in Ru-MOF (Fig. 2C). The asymmetric unit of the monoclinic Ru-MOF crystal contained one \( \{\mathsf{R u}[4,4^{\prime}\!-\!(\mathsf{H O}_{2}\mathsf{C})_{2}\!-\!\mathsf{b p y}]_{2}\mathsf{b p y}\}^{2+} \), one Zn atom, two DMF and four \( \mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O} \) molecules, i.e. \( [\mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{R u}}\mathsf{Z n}]\bullet2\mathsf{D M F}\bullet4\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O} \) (Kent et al., 2010). The pore channel in Ru-MOF formed by the coordination of carboxyl groups in \( \mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{R u}} \) and \( Z\mathsf{n}^{2+} \) was illustrated in Fig. 2D and proved by \( \mathsf{N}_{2} \) adsorption-desorption tests (Fig. S4). A comparison of the actual XRD patterns of Ru-MOF and the simulated patterns of the crystal structure confirmed that Ru-MOF was successfully synthesized (Fig. 2E).
