## 3.2 Fabrication of Ru-MOF/GO-modified electrode
The other challenge for MOF-based EC system is their insulating nature (Morozan & Jaouen, 2012, Yang et al., 2010). An efficient strategy for improving their conductivity is the use of a mixture of MOF and conductive material, such as carbon nanocrystals (Allendorf et al., 2011, Jahan et al., 2010). Graphene oxide (GO) is much efficient for the preparation of MOF-modified electrode because of its high stability and hydrophilicity (Fig. S5-S7 and Table S2). On the contrary, reduced GO (RGO) was found to be easy-of-aggregation and was diffcult to mix with Ru-MOF homogeneously for the preparation of Ru-MOF modified electrode because of hydrophilic property of RGO (Fig. S6). GO was thus used to attach Ru-MOF to electrode surface by \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking (Zhang et al., 2010, Li et al., 2013, Wu et al., 2014) and to improve the electron conductivity of the MOF coating (Allendorf et al., 2011, Jahan et al., 2010). The results in Fig. S7 confirmed the efficiency of GO for the immobilization of Ru-MOF on the electrode surface.
The fabrication and test of Ru-MOF/GO-modified electrode was illustrated in Scheme 1. The use of Ru-MOF improve the cost efficiency of EC and ECL application substantially because only \(5\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of a mixture of \(25\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) GO (125 \(\upmu\mathfrak{g})\) and 5 mg mL-1 Ru-MOF \((25~\upmu\mathfrak{g},~25~\mathsf{n m o l})\) was required to prepare an MOF-modified electrode. The \(\mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{R u}}\) content of \(74.3\ \%\) in Ru-MOF was higher than that in previous systems to improve the photoelectric efficiency (Hu & Xu, 2010, Miao et al., 2002, Richter, 2004, Yin et al., 2004). Fluorescence was used to reveal the leakage of ruthenium MOF. No obvious fluorescence was observed from the solution soaking with the MOF-modified electrode (Fig. S7E). The result confirmed that the MOF was stable enough for the preparation of its modified electrode and for ECL applications.
## 3.3 Electrochemistry and ECL of Ru-MOF
3.3 Electrochemistry and ECL of Ru-MOFThe electrochemical activity of Ru-MOF was confirmed using the MOF-modified electrode over the potential range of -1.5 V–+1.7 V. The peak at 1.05 V is the typical oxidation peak for \(\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}{}^{2+}\) (Fig. 3Ab). After addition of tripropylamine (TPrA) to electrolyte, the oxidative current was enhanced from 24 to \(72\ \upmu\mathsf{A}\) and the anodic peak shifted to 0.95 V (Fig. 3Ac). This behavior of Ru-MOF was consistent with the catalytic oxidation of TPrA by \(\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}{}^{3+}\) (Zu & Bard, 2000). The oxidative peak intensity was also improved from 40 to 72 \(\upmu\mathsf{A}\) in comparison with that at \(\mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{R u}}\)-modified electrode even though the same content of ECL luminophore was used (25 nmol of \(\mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{R u}}\)) (Fig. 3A). TPrA readily permeated into the well-ordered Ru-MOF, resulting in the improved mass transfer. The redox-active site in Ru-MOF was therefore more accessible for efficient electron transfer between TPrA and Ru-MOF. The structure of Ru-MOF was independent of the EC process and is therefore electrochemically stable. Thus, Ru-MOF shows great potential for EC applications because of its well-ordered structure, good stability, and high catalytic efficiency.
The preferred position for Fig. 3.
The preferred position for Fig. 3.The electrocatalysis of Ru-MOF was further confirmed by its ECL emission evaluated with TPrA as a co-reactant. The emission intensity increased linearly by at least 3 orders of magnitudes to around 10000 a.u. as TPrA concentration increased from 0 to 2 mM (Fig. 3B and 3C). However, the ECL magnitude at \(\mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{R u}}\)-modified electrode was about 6250 a.u. at 2 mM TPrA (inset of Fig. 3B) although the same amounts of ECL luminophore (25 nmol) were used for the preparation of their modified electrodes under the same test conditions. Because of the high EC-stability of Ru-MOF, the ECL responses were derived
from the redox-active \( \mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{Ru}} \) immobilized in Ru-MOF, which has the similar ECL behavior to the previous solid-state ECL systems (Richter, 2004, Hu & Xu, 2010, Yin et al., 2004, Yin, 2012, Khramov & Collinson, 2000, Wei & Wang, 2011, Xing & Yin, 2009b, Choi et al., 2003, Sun et al., 2010, Forster & Hogan, 2000, Guo & Dong, 2004, Xing & Yin, 2009a). Moreover, a higher emission was recorded from the MOF-modified electrode, but a relatively greater linear slope of TPrA was observed at \( \mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{Ru}} \)-modified electrode (Fig. 3C).
## 3.4 ECL properties of Ru-MOF
ECL emission from Ru-MOF modified electrode can be divided into direct and surface emissions. The former is derived from the oxidation of TPrA entering into the framework of Ru-MOF and interacting with \( \mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{Ru}}^{3+} \) directly for electron transfer. The surface emission results from the catalytic oxidation of TPrA by \( \mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{Ru}}^{3+} \) with GO as medium. As illustrated in Fig. S8, the phase diffusion and surface reaction of TPrA govern the direct emission, which therefore reflects the effect of the size and charge of co-reactants on the diffusion in Ru-MOF and their ECL efficiency. Surface emission is dependent mainly on the concentration and ECL efficiency of a co-reactant. If a co-reactant has a low ECL emission at low concentration because of its low diffusion capacity into Ru-MOF, it may have a relatively greater slope because diffusion has less effect on surface emission at high concentration level and ECL is mainly concentration-dependent. At low concentrations, the greater accessibility of TPrA to the well-ordered MOF attributes to the greater direct emission, while surface emission becomes prominent at high TPrA concentrations. Attributing to this, a higher emission is observed at the MOF-modified electrode, whereas a relatively greater linear slope is found from the \( \mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{Ru}} \)-modified electrode. The ECL results confirmed the high permeability and efficient mass transfer of Ru-MOF. Thus, the redox of ruthenium complex, electron transfer and pore
channel played an important role in the ECL process of the MOF-modified electrode.
The ECL emission stability was tested by submitting the modified electrodes to consecutive potential scan with TPrA as a co-reactant. The precisions were 3.9 and \(5.1\%\) (RSD, \(\mathsf{n}=8\)) at MOF- and \(\mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{Ru}}\)-modified electrodes, respectively (Fig. 4A and 4B). High emission stability also indicated that Ru-MOF is stable enough for the ECL process. Because of its good linear response and high stability, the ECL behavior and properties of Ru-MOF were then investigated with different ECL co-reactants, such as TPrA, triethylamine (TEA), and oxalate.
The preferred position for Fig. 4.
ECL emission is produced on concomitant oxidation of Ru-MOF and co-reactants, such as TPrA, which first diffuses to electrode surface from solution phase (Fig. S8). Ru-MOF is electrochemically oxidized to form ECL emission with TPrA, TEA, and oxalate as co-reactants. At low concentrations, nearly the same ECL intensity was observed for both TEA and TPrA although TEA has a lower ECL efficiency (Fig. 4C) because TEA readily permeated into Ru-MOF to improve direct ECL emission attributed by its molecular size. The high diffusion of TEA in Ru-MOF offsets its low ECL efficiency in comparison with those of TPrA (Richter, 2004, Miao et al., 2002) at their low concentration. Similarly, Wang and Lin (Wang & Lin, 2011) observed the size-dependent diffusion behavior of various amines in an MOF prepared with \(\{\mathsf{Ru}[4,4^{\prime}-(\mathsf{HO}_{2}\mathsf{C})_{2}-\mathsf{bpy}]\mathsf{bpy}_{2}\}^{2+}\) and \(\mathsf{Zn}^{2+}\) via luminescence quenching test. We provided the ECL evidence of the different diffusion of TPrA and TEA in the present Ru-MOF. Because surface emission is main contributor at high
co-reactant concentrations, TEA exhibited a lower linear slope for ECL than TPrA, attributed to low ECL efficiency.
Oxalate is negative and is difficult in permeating the negative framework of Ru-MOF. Owing to this, the lowest emission for oxalate was observed even at a high concentration in combination of its low ECL efficiency, which verified the charge selectivity of Ru-MOF (Fig. 4Cc). With the increase of concentration, oxalate exhibited a high slope because the surface emission effect became dominant with concentration-dependent mode. The different ECL behaviors of co-reactants at Ru-MOF modified electrode were therefore used to illustrate the well-defined structure, permselectivity, charge selectivity and catalytic selectivity of Ru-MOF toward those co-reactants. All of the selectivity results from unique structure of Ru-MOF, but was difficult to be observed in other Ru-immobilized systems (Richter, 2004, Hu & Xu, 2010, Yin et al., 2004, Yin, 2012, Khramov & Collinson, 2000, Wei & Wang, 2011, Xing & Yin, 2009b, Choi et al., 2003, Sun et al., 2010, Forster & Hogan, 2000, Guo & Dong, 2004, Xing & Yin, 2009a).
## 3.5 ECL mechanism at Ru-MOF/GO-modified electrode
The ECL emission from Ru-MOF is governed by oxidation-reduction excitation mechanism (Richter, 2004, Miao et al., 2002). TPrA oxidation is critical for the formation of highly active intermediate. This intermediate reacts with \(\mathsf{R u}^{3+}\)-MOF to produce an excited state through hot electron \((\mathsf{e}_{\mathsf{h o t}}^{-})\) injection (Richter, 2004, Miao et al., 2002). TPrA oxidation can occur via homogeneous catalytic oxidation by the electrogenerated \(\mathsf{R u}^{3+}\)-MOF or direct oxidation on electrode surface (Miao et al., 2002). Under suitable conditions (mainly determined by the electrode surface characteristics), direct TPrA oxidation achieves a low potential ECL because the oxidized potential of TPrA is approximately 0.8 V (Hu & Xu, 2010, Miao et al., 2002, Richter, 2004, Yin et al., 2004). However, the ECL emission peak from Ru-MOF appeared at 1.21 V
with the onset beyond 1.05 V (Fig. 3B). This result suggested that direct TPrA oxidation is difficult possibly because the phase diffusion is required (Fig. S8). Thus, we concluded that the ECL emission is achieved mainly with the catalytic TPrA oxidation by \(\mathsf{R u}^{3+}\)-MOF.
The energy and electron transfer in Ru-MOF/GO composite was evaluated using fluorescence quenching test. Respectively, the maximum emission of \(\mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{R u}}\) and Ru-MOF was observed at 689 and 671 nm while GO has weak emission (Fig. S9). The fluorescence from Ru-MOF was quenched from 6332 a.u. to 283.1 a.u. by GO with a 25 nm red-shift in the maximum wavelength (Fig. S9A). Although quenching to \(\mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{R u}}\) fluorescence (from 2411 to 202 a.u.) occurred after mixing with GO, only a 2 nm red-shift in the maximum wavelength was observed (Fig. S9B). These results indicated that the distance between Ru-MOF and GO was sufficiently short to suit the energy tunneling for Förster resonance energy transfer different to the bulk \(\mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{R u}}\) (Leong et al., 2014). Consequently, Ru-MOF was readily oxidized through electron tunneling with GO as a medium (Fig. S8). The ECL mechanism and related electron flow were therefore proposed as follows (D: electron donor; A: electron acceptor) (Miao et al., 2002, Richter, 2004, Zu & Bard, 2000).
\[
[\mathrm{Ru}^{2+}-\mathrm{MOF}]-\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow[\mathrm{Ru}^{3+}-\mathrm{MOF}]
\]
\[
\mathrm{D}^{2+}-\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow\mathrm{A}^{3+}
\]
\[
[\mathrm{Ru}^{3+}-\mathrm{MOF}]+\mathrm{TPrA}\rightarrow[\mathrm{Ru}^{2+}-\mathrm{MOF}]+\mathrm{TPrA}^{+}
\]
\[
\mathrm{A}^{3+}+\mathrm{D}\rightarrow\mathrm{D}^{2+}+\mathrm{A}^{+}
\]
\[
\mathrm{TPrA^{+*}}\rightarrow\mathrm{TPrA^{*}}+H^{+}
\]
\[
[\mathrm{Ru}^{3+}-\mathrm{MOF}]+\mathrm{TPrA}^{*}\rightarrow[\mathrm{Ru}^{2+*}-\mathrm{MOF}]+Product
\]
\[
\mathrm{A}^{3+}+\mathrm{D}\rightarrow\mathrm{D}^{2+*}+Product
\]
\[
[\mathrm{Ru}^{2+*}-\mathrm{MOF}]\rightarrow[\mathrm{Ru}^{2+}-\mathrm{MOF}]+hv
\]
\[
\mathrm{D}^{2+*}\rightarrow\mathrm{D}^{2+}+hv
\]
ECL emission is produced upon the concomitant oxidation of Ru-MOF and TPrA. \(\mathsf{R u}^{2+}\)-MOF is oxidized electrochemically to form \(\mathsf{R u}^{3+}\)-MOF with GO as a media (Equation 1). Electrogenerated \([\mathsf{R u}^{3+}\)-MOF] oxidizes TPrA catalytically to form the active radical, \(\mathsf{T P r A}^{+\bullet}\) (Equation 2). The short-lived radical cation \(\mathsf{T P r A}^{+\bullet}\) loses a proton from α-carbon to form the strongly reducing intermediate, \(\mathsf{T P r A}^{\bullet}\) (Richter, 2004). This radical injects a hot electron \((\mathsf{e}_{\mathsf{n o t}}^{-})\) into another \(\mathsf{R u}^{3+}\)-MOF species to form excited \([\mathsf{R u}^{2+}\)-MOF], which emits a photon (Equation 4 and 5) (Miao et al., 2002, Richter, 2004). \([\mathsf{R u}^{2+}\)-MOF] is regenerated after an emission cycle (Equation 5 and Fig. S8), making Ru-MOF advantageous for photoelectric translating using a simple instrumentation setup (Guo & Dong, 2004).
With respect to the electron flow, Ru-MOF, as the electron donor, first loses an electron electrochemically (Equation 1). The electrogenerated \([\mathsf{R u}^{3+}\)-MOF] as an acceptor, then oxidizes TPrA to form \(\mathsf{T P r A}^{+\bullet}\) through one-electron \((1e^{-})\) transfer. The cation radical \(\mathsf{T P r A}^{+\bullet}\) rearranges intramolecularly to form the highly reactive intermediate TPrA (Equation 2). Another electrogenerated \([\mathsf{R u}^{3+}\)-MOF], as an acceptor, reacts with the radical through hot electron \((\mathsf{e}_{\mathsf{n o t}}^{-})\) injection to form excited state \([\mathsf{R u}^{2+}\)-MOF]. Although two \([\mathsf{R u}^{3+}\)-MOF] species are related to one photon emission, \([\mathsf{R u}^{2+}\)-MOF] is regenerated for the next emission cycle for high sensitivity (Miao et al., 2002, Richter, 2004). Notably, the structure of Ru-MOF is independent of the ECL process: \(\mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{R u}}^{2+}\to\mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{R u}}^{3+}\to\mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{R u}}^{2+}\to\mathsf{L}_{\mathsf{R u}}^{2+}\), and thus Ru-MOF does not collapse and is highly stable for ECL emission.
## 3.6 Real application of the MOF-based ECL system
Tertiary amine compounds have a high ECL efficiency for Ru-complexes, we thus confirm the real application of the MOF system with the detection of cocaine (Zhao et al., 2011). As shown in Fig. 5A, the higher cocaine concentration resulted in the greater ECL emission with a
concentration-dependent response. A good linear response was observed from the logarithm of ECL intensity and the logarithm of cocaine concentration (Fig. 5B). The high ECL stability of the MOF system with cocaine was validated via five cyclic voltammetric determinations (Fig. S10).
The preferred position for Fig. 5.
To investigate the feasibility of Ru-MOF system for real applications, the stability of the modified electrode in serum was tested. After the analysis of serum sample with the Ru-MOF modified electrode, the ECL emission from the serum was used to test the released Ru complex. As shown in Fig. S11, no apparent ECL emission was observed with and without 20 mM TPrA, indicating that Ru-MOF was stably attached on the electrode surface during electrochemical process. The concentration of cocaine in the serum sample was obtained with standard addition method. The cocaine recoveries of 99.7-103 % and reproducibility of 6.2-8.6 % (RSD, \( n = 3 \)) were observed as shown in Table S3. All of the results confirmed the efficiency of the system for the determination of cocaine in serum sample.
## 4. Conclusions
In the summary, we demonstrated the first example for generating ECL signal from an MOF prepared from a redox-active complex. The pore channel played an important role in the ECL process of MOF as validated by the higher ECL emission than that at \( L_{Ru} \)-modified electrode. The rapid, efficient electron transfer and porous structure of Ru-MOF were confirmed by evaluating ECL responses in the presence of various co-reactants. With such unique properties and good concentration-dependent ECL responses, the
determination of cocaine in the serum sample confirms that Ru-MOF will be a good platform for the sensing purpose. Importantly, the study of Ru-MOF with ECL capability will be helpful to better understand the relationship among MOF, co-reactants and electrodes and highlights the potential of MOFs as a novel platform for the design of electrochemical systems.
